 less to worry  about but for olga there is guilt as  well and a longing to help her homeland  but i'm doing what i can  honestly i'm praying every second  that they will survive calgary's elena  burden is in poland she met her sister  at the border with ukraine for just a  moment this week long enough for a hug  and a promise  that she would take her eight and  ten-year-old niece and nephew back to  canada where they would be safe my  sister  tanya  so she said i can give you my kids hand  to hand  because i trust you you can take care  about them  but  i will go back to ukraine an impossible  decision facing so many mothers in the  country right now to leave with their  kids or bring them to safety before  returning to fight women are involved in  every  conceivable aspect of this from from the  front lines to to being the refugees  themselves to caring for the refugees  32 year old alana bashinska has become a  soldier as well  in her old life she taught people how to  use makeup now she is defending her city  it's my country it's my cities my here  are my families and my friends  my animals  so why should i wait until someone will  rescue me so alana fights as elena opens  her home and ogre advocates and prays  just three stories in a country with  millions more  heather urex west global news calgary  and that is global national for this  tuesday i'm donna friesen we leave you  tonight with some melodies in the  darkness  that's ukrainian violinist vera  litovchenka she's playing in a bomb  shelter in her kiev this performance she  says is dedicated to her late  grandmother she plays to lift the  spirits of those sheltering here and  when her friends react to her posts on  social media she says she knows they're  still alive  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow  [Music]
 

